<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nathdwara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nathdwara is a town in the Rajsamand district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, about 45 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, which grew around the temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrinathji">Shrinathji</a>. The image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> was brought there in 1672 from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> region, to protect it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s hostility, under the protection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharana">Maharana</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raj-singh-i">Raj Singh I</a>. The temple is the chief shrine of the Pushtimarg tradition, and the town is known for its pichhwai temple paintings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nathdwara.jpg" alt="Nathdwara" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kunwar Nadaan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vishwas_Swaroopam_after_completion_of_work.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
